WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.388 --> 00:00:01.420
(plucky music)

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.720
- [Narrator] We visited the Los Angeles

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.610
based band La Santa Cecilia

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.900
at their concert in New York to talk about

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.260
their single, "Winning",
which uses elements

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.320
from Bachata as background for a poem

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.370
about the evils of social media.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.000
- I wear rainbows as skirts,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.820
two cowboys build a fire,

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.430
there's a guy eating a flower--

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.250
- Describing "Winning" musically, huh?

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.560
- Yeah, it's like a, like a pop-rock,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.580
Bachata, spoken word.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.990
- Punk rock.
- Punky, rocky song.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.280
- So what is Bachata?

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.440
- Bachata is a Latin American music genre

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.080
from the Dominican republic that is

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.590
rising in popularity across the world.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.730
Just look at bachatero Romeo Santos,

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.955
the face of Bachata for many.

00:00:39.955 --> 00:00:43.788
(singing in foreign language)

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.500
He has billions of views on YouTube,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.550
and he's performed a sold
out show to 80,000 fans

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.680
at New Jersey's MetLife Stadium.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.380
I have to admit I had never
heard of Bachata before

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.320
and especially with the genre's blurring,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.390
it's kind of hard to tell what
exactly is Bachata or not.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.120
Later on I'm going to attempt to create

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.590
my own Bachata track, but
first let's break down

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:08.438
the musical DNA of what
makes Bachata, Bachata.

00:01:08.438 --> 00:01:11.105
(bachata music)

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.990
We interviewed La Santa Cecilia about

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.940
how Bachata has inspired them.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.390
This is what they have to say.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.920
- To me Bachata is like, like, a bolero,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.840
and I grew up singing boleros with Pepe,

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.110
that's, I mean, that's what we
played when we were younger.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.380
All these beautiful romantic ballads,

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.370
and he plays a requinto and,

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.816
but it's also very
(speaking foreign language),

00:01:30.816 --> 00:01:32.920
it's traditional, right, too so...

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.170
- Definitely traditional.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.128
It's like a Caribbean kind of rhythm,

00:01:37.128 --> 00:01:38.024
- Yeah, yeah!

00:01:38.024 --> 00:01:39.823
I mean, there's a lot of artists like

00:01:39.823 --> 00:01:43.210
Juan Luis Guerra who has
been an influence for us,

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.940
and it's just like a
danceable kind of rhythm

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.453
that we're used to.

00:01:48.453 --> 00:01:50.910
- It's music that's played in cantinas

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:55.790
where people are drinking
and feeling things in a very,

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.470
like, sensual, sometimes passionate,

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.790
very meaningful way,

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.670
so, it's like music that
men listen to and cry

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.030
and dance, and, you know,

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.680
it's kind of one of those kind of

00:02:10.291 --> 00:02:11.124
like Bohemia kind of things

00:02:11.124 --> 00:02:13.042
that inspires us a lot in the band.

00:02:13.042 --> 00:02:14.871
(slow Bachata music) Yeah,
because the, the main feature

00:02:14.871 --> 00:02:16.480
on the verses is a spoken word part,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.360
and then, and then if
you're listening carefully,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.900
you'll notice that there's a Bachata

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.582
rhythm underneath the verse.

00:02:21.582 --> 00:02:23.400
(all laughing)

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.450
- Can I borrow those?

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.200
Come on, come on!

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.033
Maracas.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:29.330
- Thanks, appreciate it.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:30.561
- Thanks, baby.

00:02:30.561 --> 00:02:33.144
(bright music)

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.270
- So where did Bachata come from?

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.120
It was born in the Dominican Republic,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.560
and for much of its history,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.930
it wasn't held in such high regard.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.900
In those days, Bachata songs used

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.258
acoustic instruments, like this.

00:02:47.258 --> 00:02:51.003
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:55.322
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:02:55.322 --> 00:02:59.739
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.070
From 1930 until 196one

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.260
the Dominican Republic
was under the control

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.570
of dictator Rafael Trujillo.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:08.930
During his three decade rule,

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.620
Trujillo censored a lot
of the creative arts,

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.240
and only sanctioned music was recorded.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.420
During this time, however,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.150
a live music scene flourished,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.580
and this is where Bachata was born.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.200
Within six months of Trujillo's
assassination in 196one

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.280
the first Bachata track was recorded

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.700
by Jose Manuel Calderon.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.920
These early Bachata songs used equipment

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.690
recovered from government radio stations.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.523
Listen to this early track
by Rafael Encarnacion.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:40.887
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:49.240
This song has a one, six, two,
five, one chord progression

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.870
which is a common chord progression

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.780
found in Bachata music.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.650
At the time, though, the music wasn't

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.610
yet known as Bachata,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.690
instead it was considered
to be a form of bolero,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.120
which is an older, Latin style of music

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.960
that has similarities to a jazz ballad.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.060
A defining feature of
this is the bass line

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.305
with this type of rhythm.

00:04:09.305 --> 00:04:12.388
(bright piano music)

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.877
Which emphasizes the, and three, four,

00:04:16.877 --> 00:04:20.437
(bright piano music)

00:04:20.437 --> 00:04:24.854
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:29.350
These uniquely Dominican bolero songs

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:30.690
were given the name Bachata

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.560
by the upper class to disparage the music.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.660
The word Bachata is Caribbean
Spanish for an informal party.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.710
During this time, Bachata music was often

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:42.990
associated with poverty,
crime, and sensuality,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.380
and found little air time
on the radio or television.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.760
Another ingredient in
Bachata is the rhythm,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.460
commonly known as El
Martillo, or The Hammer.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.100
Commonly played on the Bongos,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.550
it has a lot in common with
the Bongo rhythm of bolero.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.500
Unlike most rhythms, it emphasizes

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.530
the four instead of the one.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.113
(bright music)

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.150
When Bachata modernized, its guitar solos

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:11.860
began to employ Mambos,

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.120
or short, repeating rhythmic melodies.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.270
You can also call them grooves.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.870
Bachata is music intended for dancing.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.750
Where old fashioned Bachatas
were danced more slowly,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.240
the introduction of these Mambos

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.680
made it possible for
dancers to enjoy Bolero's

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.200
sensual romanticism, and
Meringue's upbeat energy

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.640
within the same song.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.640
Listen for the mambos
played by Antony Santos,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.870
a defining figure in the evolution

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.156
of modern, electric Bachata.

00:05:39.156 --> 00:05:41.931
(bright music)

00:05:41.931 --> 00:05:45.620
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.970
Another important aspect of modern Bachata

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:51.430
is a rhythmic pattern commonly
played by the lead guitar.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:52.940
It's arpeggiating triads,

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:57.040
and it goes one, two, three,
one, two, three, two, three.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.770
One, two, three, one,
two, three, two, three.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.370
It's unique because it's dividing

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.420
the group of four quarter note beats

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.560
into two groups of three eighth notes.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.290
One, two, three, one, two, three.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:10.420
Plus a group of two eighth notes,

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:11.520
Two, three.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.000
One, two, three, one,
two, three, two, three.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.260
And as you can hear,

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.235
it's naturally emphasizing
the fourth beat.

00:06:18.235 --> 00:06:22.652
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:27.930
That arpeggiation has become a
signature element in Bachata.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.966
Listen to it in Medicina De
Amor, by Raulin Rodriguez.

00:06:31.966 --> 00:06:36.383
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:41.210
In the 1990's, Bachata went
through another big shift.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.980
Aventura, a New York
band led by Romeo Santos,

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.110
fused Bachata with Hip Hop, R&amp;B, and Rock,

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.630
and even sprinkled
English into the lyrics.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.873
The result was a new
urban style of Bachata.

00:06:52.873 --> 00:06:57.290
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:00.700
With Aventura's success,

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:04.700
Bachata became an overnight,
world-wide pop sensation.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:08.040
The urban style of Bachata,
centered around New York City,

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.380
has since dominated over Bachata's

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.880
expanding, world-wide audience.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.400
Many fans of Bachata outside
of the Dominican Republic

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.190
don't even realize that
other forms of it exist.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:20.860
So what's next for Bachata?

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.650
It's not going to stop
evolving, that's for sure.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.230
Nowadays, artists are recording

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.870
bachatas also in English and Italian,

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.330
and many are reinterpreting pop songs

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.260
from various traditions
with Bachata rhythms

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.000
For example, another
bachatero from New York City,

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.390
Prince Royce, has a major hit

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:42.564
of a Bachata version of
"Stand By Me" by Ben E. King

00:07:42.564 --> 00:07:46.064
♪ When the night has come ♪

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.970
Some Bachata fusions
are more experimental.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.600
Let's go back to "Winning",
by La Santa Cecilia.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.740
Upon first listen, it sounds nothing like

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.990
the bachatas we've listened to so far.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.720
But it contains many of the
elements that we mentioned.

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.480
For example, it has the one, two, three,

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.010
one, two, three, two, three

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.750
arepeggiated pattern
played by the guitars.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:09.100
It also has the one six, two,
five, one chord progression

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.788
and there's even a bongo
playing a Bachata beat.

00:08:11.788 --> 00:08:16.595
♪ No filter, chocolate,
strawberry shakes, follow me ♪

00:08:16.595 --> 00:08:18.136
♪ New year send me ♪

00:08:18.136 --> 00:08:20.610
(lively singing in foreign language)

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:24.668
♪ GoFundMe, your post has been deleted ♪

00:08:24.668 --> 00:08:28.227
♪ #Winning ♪

00:08:28.227 --> 00:08:33.227
♪I can find no inspiration,
neverending information ♪

00:08:35.162 --> 00:08:38.149
♪ Always stuck in purgatory ♪

00:08:38.149 --> 00:08:41.400
♪ 'Cause I'm addicted to your story ♪

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.247
In the spirit of fusion
I'm going to remix the song

00:08:43.247 --> 00:08:45.930
"Kriye Kriye" into a chiptune track

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.620
inspired by retro video game music.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.254
I think a lot of the patterns
and progressions used,

00:08:51.254 --> 00:08:52.150
(keyboard notes chiming)

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.661
will fit really nicely
with this kind of sound.

00:08:54.661 --> 00:08:58.661
(lively retro video game music)

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.610
If you liked this episode and want more,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.510
don't forget to subscribe and
